> Well, my point is that I have no problems with a mechanism that
> unlimited loot in return for an equal amount of tedium, but people who
> want to use it should have to actually experience at least *some* of
> the tedium. Quid pro quo and all that :)

Using tedium as a deterrent isn't always the best approach to design,
since some players' tolerance for tedium is far higher than others...
and some players' spare time is significantly greater than others.

Witness again the June tournament.  78291 put up 42 or so ascensions in
the month.  Why?  He played between 8 and 9 hours a day, and maintained
about a 50% ratio.

I don't intend to detract from this achievement at all, but how many
other of the players who are capable of maintaining a 50% ratio could do
this?  It'll be close to zero, I can assure you, because most of those
players simply don't have 8-9 spare hours a day, every day, for a month
to play.  I can guarantee that I don't, nor would I want to if I did.

So what does this all demonstrate?  Well, first, that the 'most
ascensions' trophy is not necessarily representative of what one would
think it's representative of; it now reads "most spare time".  Which is
probably not what the trophy was intended to be... but, it didn't take
into account all the factors.

This is how a scoring system can be broken by just assuming that people
"won't do" certain things.  Some people always will, and in some cases
it can render that score meaningless.

